And as Jesus went on from there, he saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax office. And he said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Matthew. Many tax collectors and sinners, who were not regarded as righteous by the Pharisees and Sadducees, came and sat down with Jesus and the disciples, and they all ate together.
The Pharisees saw this and said to the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that. And He said, "Those who are healthy are not in need of a physician, but rather those who are ill."
Go and learn what this phrase means: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice." *Hosea 6:6* "I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came to him and said, "Rabbi, we and the Pharisees fast often, yet the disciples of yours do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the groomsmen mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is still with them, they cannot mourn. But the hour is coming when all shall be taken from them, and then they will fast."
People do not patch a new garment with pieces cut from an old, worn garment, because the new piece would be snatched away from the old one and the patch would be ruined.
People will not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst, spilling the wine and ruining both the wine and the wineskins. Instead, people will pour new wine into new wineskins, preserving both.
While he was speaking this, there came one of the rulers of the synagogue, Jairus, and fell down at Jesus' feet, imploring Him, "My little daughter is at the point of death! But come and lay Your hand on her, and she will live!"
And Jesus rose and the disciples followed him.
And immediately a woman who had an issue of blood for twelve years came and stood behind Him, and touched the border of His garment. And immediately her issue of blood stopped.
"I need only touch his clothes, then I shall be cured," she thought to herself.
And Jesus turned and saw her, and said to her, "Woman, your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed in that moment.
And when Jesus entered the house of the leader of the Pharisees, he saw there the musicians. The presence of a large crowd of people was annoying.
He said to them, "Go, the girl is not dead, she is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
And after He had put the crowd outside, Jesus went in. And He took her by the hand, and He called her, and immediately she stood up.
News of what Jesus did spread throughout the region. Jesus healing two blind men.
And during that time, two blind beggars followed him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, O son of David!"
And when Jesus entered the house, the blind approached him and he said to them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" They said, "Yes, Lord!"
Then he touched their eyes and said, "According to your faith, be it done to you."
Jesus said to them very earnestly, "See that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that area.
As they were coming out, a man was brought to him who was possessed, by a demon. But he did not speak.
And when the demon was driven out, the man spoke. And the crowd was astonished, and they said, "We have never seen anything like this in all Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "It is by the prince of demons that he drives out demons." There are few who are workers.
And Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every weakness.
When he saw the crowds, he was deeply moved for them. They were like sheep without a shepherd, being scattered and beaten.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few.
So, seek from the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest.
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. Starting with Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
The following after that are Philip and Bartholomew, and likewise Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. And two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Judas the Zealot.
The last of them were Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will betray Jesus to his enemies later on.
Jesus sent the twelve out with instructions as follows: "Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not.
Go to the house of Israel instead, for they are like lost sheep.
In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Say,
Heal the sick. Raise the dead to life. Cleanse the lepers. Drive out demons. You received freely, give freely.
Do not carry with you gold or silver or copper in your baggage.
Do not take a bag for the trip. Do not carry extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker is given what he needs.
If you enter a city or a village, look for someone who is willing to take you in as a guest. Stay at their home until you leave.
In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. When you enter the house, greet its people.
If that house welcomes you, greet it with peace. And if it does not, then do not greet it.
Not everyone will welcome you or listen to your words. And if they don't, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. It will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah on the Day of Judgment than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves; so be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
Beware! They will hand you over to local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be presented to the governors and kings because of me. You are witnesses for me before them and before those other than the Jews.
But when they hand you over, do not be anxious beforehand about what you will say or how you will say it. For at that time you will be given what to say.
It will not be you speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their sons. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
If people attack you somewhere, flee to another place. What I am about to tell you is true: you will not finish even a circuit of the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
A student is not better than his teacher. And a servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for a disciple to be like his master, and a servant like his master. If the head of the house is called Beelzebul, what will the rest of the people in the house be called?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed, and what is hidden will be brought to light.
What I am telling you in the darkness, speak of it in the light of day. What is whispered in your ear, shout of it from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Aren't the garden birds sold for two pennies? One of them doesn't fall to the ground without your father knowing.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
So do not fear, for you are more numerous than when at the bird, the stone.
What about someone who says before people, 'I know him'? I will say before my Father who is in the heavens, 'I know you.'
And as for the one who says to his brother, "Ra's, I will be for you, before my Father who is in the heavens," then I say to him, "Woe to you, I do not know you!"
Do not suppose that I have come to cast peace upon the earth; I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make the son stand against his father, and the daughter against her mother. Likewise, the daughters-in-law against their mothers-in-law.
And a man's enemies are those of his own household." (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves a father or a mother more than me is not worthy of me; and whoever loves a son or a daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me cannot be my disciple.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses it for my sake will find it.
He who has received you has received me, and he who has received me has received the One who sent me.
Indeed, the one who whispers in the breast of man, that he may obey the Most Merciful, and not obey the two effeminate ones; his reward is with the Most Merciful. And thus do We facilitate him for whom We will facilitate; and you will not find for Our sentence a change or a turning away.
Assume that someone gives, even if it is only one cup of cold water, to a little child who is following me. What I am going to tell you is a fact. That person will surely be rewarded."
Jesus and John (upon them be peace).
And after Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples, he went out teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard about the works of Christ, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for another?"
Jesus answered and said to them, "Go back to John and he will tell you what he hears and what he sees."
The blind can see, the lame can walk, the lepers are cured, the deaf can hear, the dead rise, and the poor have good news.
Blessed are those who do not disdain to serve me and who do not lose faith in me.
Jesus said to the crowds, speaking about John and his disciples leaving: "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
So, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. For people who wear fine clothes are in kings' palaces.
Why then have you come out to look? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one spoken of in the books: "I will send my angel, and he will prepare the way before me" (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. No one who has ever lived is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he was.
From the days of John the Baptist, the Queen of Heaven advances with power. And the strong are those who grasp her.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, John is the living one who was supposed to come.
Let those who have ears hear.
The example of people today is like the example of the wombs that are guided by the children in the cradle saying "Come" to wipe away a Khorassani or to caress the thighs of the planets.
