Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls. For my yoke is easy and my burden is light.
Become my slaves and learn from me, for I am gentle and do not boast. You will find rest for yourselves.
My service is easy and my provisions are light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking in the field. And his disciples, while plucking some ears of grain, were rubbing them with their hands and eating them.
Therefore the Pharisees saw it, and they said to Jesus, "Behold, your disciples do what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath."
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God, he and his men, and they ate the holy bread, which was not permitted to be eaten except by the priests.
Have you not read the law? It says how the priests in the temple are to do their work on the Sabbath day. But they are not considered to be guilty.
I am telling you that there is someone who is greater than the temple here.
The book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. And if you did not, you would not have leveled accusations against the innocent.
"The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath."
From there, Jesus went to their temple.
There was a man whose right hand was withered, crooked, and useless. The Pharisees were watching Jesus to see if they could catch him doing something on the Sabbath. They asked him, "Is it lawful to do good or to do evil on the Sabbath? Is it lawful to save life or to destroy it?"
He said to them, "Suppose one of your sheep falls into a well on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you immediately pull it out and free it?"
A man is greater in value than sheep! That is why the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, just as sound as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all things within the grasp of the Pharisees, so he departed from there. A great multitude followed him, and he healed all the sick people of the common people.
But he warned them not to reveal his identity.
This was to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah:
Behold, my servant whom I uphold, my chosen one in whom my soul delights. I have put my Spirit upon him; he will proclaim justice to the nations.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bending branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will fix everything.
The nations put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebub
