Jesus answered, "Who is my mother and who are my brothers?"
And Jesus pointed to his disciples, saying, "Behold, my mother and my brethren!
He who does the will of my Father who is in heaven, he is my brother and sister and mother.
The Farmer's Story
And on the same day, Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him, so he boarded a boat and sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using parables. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
He sowed the seed in the ground. Some of them fell on the road, so birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried out because they had no roots.
Other seed fell among the thorns; and the thorns grew up and choked the plants.
And some of them in good ground bring forth fruit, going up and increasing: one of them yields a hundred, and another sixty, and another thirty.
Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.
The disciples came to him and said, "Why do you speak to the people in parables?"
He said: "For you, it has been given, with the knowledge of the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens. As for those who are outside, that has not been given to them.
To whoever has that kind of knowledge, more will be given. In fact, they will have abundance. And to whoever does not have that knowledge, even the little that they think they have will be taken away from them.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I tell them, "They look but do not really see. They listen but do not truly hear or understand."
In them shall be fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: 'You shall indeed hear, but understand not; and see, perceive not.'
Their hearts have become hardened. So they do not listen with their ears. And they have made their eyes' sight a cover, so they do not see with it. And their ears they have made like a barrier. Will they not then look behind them and see that they are brought before their Lord? Perhaps if they did that, they would return.
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and your ears, for they hear.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you are seeing. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you are hearing. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the peasant's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes and steals what was sown in their hearts. Those people are like seed sown along the path.
And others the rosary which is in the places of the stony ones, who hear the word and immediately when they hear they receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So their faith does not last long. They quickly deviate from the religion when hardship or suffering comes because of the message.
And others fell into the rocky soil; these are the ones who hear the Word, and immediately the cares of this life and the deceitfulness of riches choke the Word, and it proves unfruitful.
And just as some plants dropped their seeds in suitable soil, these people heard the word and understood it. They are bearing fruit: you see some of them giving thirty, some sixty, some a hundredfold for each crop, as a kind of produce. The story of the harmful, ravenous weeds.
He said to the crowd, for example, another saying, "Thus is the Kingdom of Heaven. It is like a man who sowed good seed in his field."
And while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. He sowed weeds among the wheat, and then went away.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
His servants came to him and said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where, then, have these weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy has done that." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"The owner replied, 'You will simply remove the grass, and with it, you will remove the wheat.'"
Let both grow together until the harvest. And in the time of the harvest I say to the reapers: Gather first the scattered spikes, and bind them into bundles for burning. Then gather the wheat itself, and store it in my barns."
And Jesus also spoke to the crowds another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds, but when it grows, it becomes the largest garden plant of all. It becomes a tree. Birds come and rest on its branches.
