And Jesus finished these parables and then went away from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. And he began there to teach people in their religious synagogue. And they were astonished and said, "From where has this man obtained this wisdom and the ability to perform miracles?"
Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary? and his brothers James and Joseph and Simon and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not happy with him at all. Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country. There is nothing greater than to be respected in your own home."
He didn't perform many miracles there because they were not believers.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time, Herod the ruler of Galilee and the lake heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! Therefore he has authority to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John. He bound him and threw him into prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of his brother Philip.
John said to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have her wife."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people because they were regarding John as a prophet.
And on Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. Herod was greatly pleased with her.
So he swore that you would surely give it to him, whatever thing you ask of him.
Her mother said to her, "What are you saying?" So the girl said to King Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large plate."
The king was very angry, but he remembered his promise and his guests for dinner, so he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was served on a large platter to the girl, who then took it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people.
