O claimants! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They only teach human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus gathered the people around Him and said to them, "Listen and understand."
What enters into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then the disciples came to him and said, "Did you know that the Pharisees, when they heard this word, were filled with rage?"
Jesus answered, "There are some seeds that are not from my heavenly Father. They are false seeds. They will be pulled up, root and branch."
Leave them alone... The Pharisees are guides for the blind. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Then explain this to us."
Don't you understand even now? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that proceed out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you 'defiled'.
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So is murder, adultery, and every other sexual immorality. Also, theft, lying, and slander.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean.' Eating without washing your hands does not make you unclean.
And Jesus left Galilee and came to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman from the land of Canaan was living near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, saying, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! For my daughter is severely possessed by a demon."
Jesus did not utter a word. The disciples approached him. They begged him, saying, "Send her away from us, for she is crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He replied, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, sir, said that woman. But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table.
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you want." And her daughter was healed at that hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus went away from there. And he walked along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up the mountain and sat down there.
Great multitudes came to him. They brought him those who were blind, lame, paralyzed, dumb, and many others. They laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. And those who were not able to speak were speaking, and the disabled were healed, and the paralyzed were walking, and the blind were seeing. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have compassion for these people, because they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way."
His disciples answered him, "We have no more than five loaves and two fish here." And they said, "Where can we buy bread to feed such a crowd?"
How many loaves do you have? Jesus asked them. They answered him, Seven, and a few small fish.
Jesus told the multitude to sit down on the ground.
And he took the seven loaves and the ears of grain, and after he had given thanks, he broke it and gave it to the disciples, and they distributed it to the crowd.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples picked up seven baskets full of the remaining pieces.
There were four thousand men who ate. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat, and set sail for the district near Magadan.
Jesus is asked to give a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came, testing Jesus and asking him to show them a sign from heaven.
He said: "In the evening look at the sky. It says: The weather will be good. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says, "It will be windy today. The sky is red and overcast." She knows the meaning of what she sees in the sky. But you do not understand the signs of what is happening now.
A bad people, who disbelieved in the signs of their Lord and the meeting of the Hereafter. They seek a sign for themselves. And they will not be given a sign, except the sign of Jonah. And the factions differed among the People of the Book.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. But they forgot to take bread with them.
Beware, Jesus said to them. "Watch out for the hypocrisy of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
And the disciples talked with one another, saying, "This is because we have no bread with us."
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Your faith is very weak! Why do you think to yourselves that you have no bread?
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for two thousand people? Don't you recall how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
Weren't you seven loaves of bread that earned the four thousand? How many baskets have you filled with leftovers?
How can you not understand? I did not speak to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Finally, the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them against the leaven of bread used for baking, but against the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter said that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They said, "Some say John the Baptist, others say Elijah, and others say Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But who do you say that I am?" he asked them.
Simon Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood have not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
This is what I tell you. You are Peter. And on this rock I will build my church. And the gates of hell will not overcome it.
I will give you the keys to the Kingdom of the Heavens. Whatever you bind on Earth will be bound in Heaven, and whatever you loose on Earth will be loosed in Heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. He spoke of his impending death.
And from that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples that which would happen to Him. And He said to them, "It is necessary for Me to go to Jerusalem." "And there they will deliver Me to the chief priests and the scribes. And they will condemn Me to death and deliver Me to the Gentiles. And they will mock Me and scourge Me and kill Me. And after three days I will rise again."
Bartholomew took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "God be merciful to you, Jesus, my Lord! This will by no means happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are not setting your mind on God's interests, but on man's."
Then Jesus spoke with his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What is the benefit if a person were to gain the whole world but lose himself? Or what else could a person give as a ransom for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and then He will reward each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those who stand here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, Jacob, and John his brother, and He led them up to a high mountain by themselves.
And his appearance changed before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became dazzling white like the light.
And suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tents here, one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was speaking with them, a bright cloud opened and enveloped them, and a voice came from the cloud saying, "This is my chosen son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him."
And when the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly afraid, and fell to the ground.
And the Lord said to Moses, "Say to the children of Israel, 'You shall be holy, for I, the Lord your God, am holy.'"
And when they looked, they saw a single man alone, who was Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And while they were coming down, Jesus told them what to do. He said to them, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen, wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. It is necessary for Elijah to come first to prepare everything.
But I tell you that Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him. They did to him whatever they pleased. So also will the Son of Man be done to."
The disciples finally understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Healing of a demon-possessed boy.
And when they came near the crowd, a man approached Jesus and knelt before him.
Sir, have mercy on my son, for he suffers from violent tremors to an extreme degree, and he often falls into the fire or water.
I brought it for your pupils, but they have not been able to heal him.
"O ye infidels and ye evildoers!" Jesus replied. "How long shall I yet be with you? And how long shall I yet suffer you? Bring ye unto me the child."
Jesus commanded the devil to come out of the boy, and he came out of him that very hour. And the boy was immediately healed.
Then the disciples approached Jesus privately and asked him, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I am about to tell you is true. If you had faith the size of a mustard seed, it would be enough. You could say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it would move. Nothing would be impossible for you."
Translated
They met in Galilee. And Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him, and on the third day, he will rise from the dead." The disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus Pays the Temple Tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived at Capernaum. In the house, tax collectors approached Peter and asked him, "Doesn't your teacher pay the tax?"
Yes, he does, he answered. And when Peter entered the house, it was Jesus who spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Whose sons do the kings of the earth collect taxes and tribute from, their own sons or foreigners?"
Peter said, "From others." And Jesus said to him, "Then the sons will not condemn."
But we don't want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them as tax for you and me.
